 { In this section we'll be discussing two important methods for combating the opponent's space advantage. These are undermining and overextension. Let's now turn to the first method of undermining. This requires spotting your opponent's plan to grab space on one side of the board, and immediately reacting in an energetic way with the intention of refuting their plan. Different tools can be used for this purpose, such as pawn breaks or sacrifices. Let's begin with an example where White failed to react to Black's queenside pawn advance in time to see how quickly things can go downhill if you do not remain alert. } 1. { -KEY- } Qc2 $6 { White ignores the invading pawn. } ( 1. b3 $1 { The pawn break would have avoided any trouble, since Black would have had to play ...cxb3 when after axb3 White has effectively exchanged their a-pawn for Black's c-pawn, ensuring them better central control. } cxb3 ( 1. Qc2 b5 $2 { fails to } 2. a4 $1 { when } a6 $2 { is not possible due to } 3. axb5 $16 ) 
2. axb3 $14 { Black's space advantage has been eliminated, leaving White with a slight edge due to having more pawns near the centre. } ) 
Nc6 2. Nbd2 $6 ( { A second inaccuracy. White needed to once again have played } 2. b3 { . } ) 
a6 { Black is preparing to advance ...b5, consolidating their space advantage. } 3. Be2 Bd6 4. O-O O-O 5. h3 $6 { A third mistake in a row. White is concerned about ...Ng4 ideas when the real problem lies on the other flank. } ( { While the ideal moment to play } 5. b3 { had already passed, it was still better than the approach taken in the game. After } b5 6. a4 Rb8 7. axb5 axb5 $15 { Black would have stood a little better with the extra space, but at least White would have had the a-file for their rook. } ) 
h6 { Black takes a moment to stop Be3-g5 ideas. A sensible approach since in general the side with more space benefits from avoiding piece exchanges. } 6. Rae1 b5 $1 { White has arranged all of their pieces in the centre and kingside, but the problem is that they lack any useful pawn breaks or piece maneuvers. On the other hand, Black will now have an easy path forward on the queenside. } 7. a3 a5 $1 { White is already close to lost, with the ...b4 pawn break likely to come next. If Black's approach seems familiar to you, it is because it is the same pawn strategy employed in the second game examined in the previous section on 'The Space Advantage'. } 
