 { Black has to be very careful here as White has a lot more space in the centre. Can you find the only move that equalises for Black? } 1... { -KEY- } b5 $1 { A well-timed temporary pawn sacrifice! If White had been given just one more move, they likely would have played a2-a4 and stopped this possibility. } 2. Bxb5 ( 2. Nxb5 { Capturing with the knight might have been slightly preferable, but Black also does not have any problems here. } Nxe4 { The exchange of Black's b-pawn for the central pawn has left White with only the d5 pawn, which does not have much of a restricting effect on the opponent's forces. Play might continue: } 3. O-O a6 4. Na3 e5 $5 { Black is now the one who seizes the centre. } 5. dxe6 { If White did not capture en passant, then Black would be the one with the space advantage. } fxe6 $11 { The game is complex and approximately balanced, since Black's greater central pawn mass is offset by their shakier pawn chain. However, the important point is that Black has succeeded in preventing a structural bind. } ) 
Nxe4 $1 { Recovering the pawn, but temporarily going down a piece! } 3. Nxe4 Qa5+ $1 { Correct! Black has destroyed the opponent's central space advantage, and is now about to recover their piece. } 4. Nfd2 $6 { White's idea is to prepare to meet ...Qxb5 with c2-c4, in order to be able to quickly castle. } ( 4. Nc3 $1 { It might have been best to force Black to part ways with their strong bishop, even if it came at the expense of damage to the structure. } Bxc3 5. bxc3 Qxb5 6. Nd2 $13 { Black has the better structure and is already castled, but on the other hand if White can play c3-c4 then they will be able to soon castle and in the long run Black's dark-squared weaknesses may prove a serious problem. The position is unclear, but approximately balanced. } ) 
Qxb5 5. c4 Qa6 6. O-O { The dust has settled, and while White still has a little more space in the centre it is not a concern. In fact, Black's bishop pair and pressure against the queenside mean that they are the side with an edge. } Nd7 7. Re1 { This natural move already lands White into a lot of trouble. } Ne5 $1 { Exposing the weaknesses in the White position. } 8. Qe2 Rb8 $17 { Black has tremendous piece pressure, and can continue to undermine White's space advantage with the ...e6 pawn break at the right moment. Our first undermining example was beginner-friendly, this one I would say is intermediate level. In our next example, we'll take a look at a game between two elite grandmasters to see a more advanced version of undermining in action. } 
