 { Taken from the online game Carlsen,M - Sarin,N (2023). Black was under pressure due to White's space advantage in the center and kingside. There was however one move that could have kept the game balanced. GM Sarin missed it, playing 1...Bd6? and quickly lost after 2.e5! , which increased Carlsen's space lead. This one is tough! But if you'd like to challenge yourself, you can pause here and try to find the move that would have kept Black out of trouble. } 1... { -KEY- } e5 $1 { An incredible resource. The pawn can be captured in three different ways, but none of them are convincing. Let's check them out. } ( 1... Bd6 $2 { was the game move, but Black got wiped out after } 2. e5 $1 Be7 3. g5 $18 { With the steamroller in full swing, there is no hope for Black's king to survive the attack. } ) 
2. Nxe5 { The most natural recapture is also the most challenging for Black to meet. } ( 2. dxe5 $6 { This move concedes the c5 square, which functions like an outpost. After } Nc5 $15 { Black has full compensation here due to the advanced outpost on d3, as well as the possibility to strike at White's weak g4 pawn with ...Bc8. } ) 
 ( 2. Bxe5 $6 { Giving up the dark-squared bishop is not a good idea. } Nxe5 3. dxe5 { If 3.Nxe5 the h4 pawn hangs. } Bc8 $15 { White's kingside pawns are in trouble against the powerful bishop pair. } ) 
Nxe5 3. Bxe5 { In the event that Nihal saw the move 17...e5, he may have discarded it based on this line. However, here Black has one move that equalises on the spot! } ( 3. dxe5 $6 Bxh4 $15 { White's structure has been slightly damaged, while material remains even. Importantly, White no longer has either the e4-e5 or g4-g5 advances that he was threatening in the starting position. If anything, Black stands slightly better here. } ) 
Nc6 $1 { White cannot hang on to both his bishop and centre. } ( { But not } 3... Bxh4 $2 { since after } 4. Bxc7 $16 { White is in command. } ) 
4. Bxc7 { The difference now is that the d4 pawn is under fire. } Rxd4 5. Rxd4 Nxd4 6. Qxc4 { Still, White is up a pawn but he is a little uncoordinated, and his advanced kingside pawns are undefended. Black can exploit these features of the position with his next move: } Qc8 $1 7. Qxd4 Qxc7 { White's extra pawn is offset by his dark-squared weaknesses and the strength of Black's bishops. This last example was very advanced and you will rarely have to solve such difficult problems to play an undermining move. Situations like the ones we saw in the previous two examples are far more common. In any case, the concept of undermining is essential when you are the side with less space. Sometimes however, a different approach is needed to fight back. We'll discuss this with our next few examples. } 
