 { The final concept that we will cover is that of 'overextension'. This happens when your opponent has a space advantage, but it's a bit shaky and you can find a way to apply a lot of piece pressure to their structure. } 1... { -KEY- } exd4 $1 { White has built up a large pawn centre, but we will soon see that their plan was too ambitious. } 2. Nxd4 { White grabs with the knight to keep control of the b4 square. } ( 2. cxd4 $2 Nb4 $1 { Black starts by winning the bishop pair. } 3. Qb3 Nxd3 4. Qxd3 Re8 $1 { The e4 pawn is doomed. } 5. Nbc3 ( 5. e5 { also loses the pawn since after } dxe5 6. fxe5 Rxe5 $19 { Black's rook cannot be captured without losing the queen. } ) 
Nxe4 $1 { A temporary piece sacrifice. } 6. Nxe4 Bf5 7. N2g3 d5 $1 8. Nxf5 dxe4 $1 9. Qd1 gxf5 $19 { The dust has settled, leaving Black a central pawn to the good. } ) 
Nxd4 $1 { Perhaps counter-intuitively, Black 'improves' White's structure. However, they have a concrete follow-up in mind. } 3. cxd4 Ng4 $1 { The key idea! White has no good way to meet the attack on d4. } 4. e5 { Shutting down the scope of the bishop, but inviting more exchanges. } ( 4. d5 $2 Bd4 5. Kh1 Nxh2 { wins on the spot, since } 6. Kxh2 { is met by } Qh4 { . } ) 
dxe5 $1 { Paving the way for a second Black piece to enter the White half of the board. } 5. dxe5 Qd4+ 6. Kh1 Rd8 $1 { By playing energetically and continuing to build up an initiative, Black does not give the opponent a chance to consolidate their space advantage. This is the best way to try to punish an opponent when you sense that they have overextended themselves. } 7. Bc4 Bf5 8. Qe2 Bd3 $1 { Simple tactics allow Black to exchange more pieces, something that typically favors the side with less space. } 9. Bxd3 Qxd3 10. Qxd3 Rxd3 11. Nc3 f6 $1 { With this move, Black strips away the last bit of the space advantage that remained on the board. } 12. exf6 Bxf6 $17 { Black stands much better due to their more active pieces and queenside pressure. } 
