 { It will soon become apparent that White has been too ambitious and overextended themselves, resulting in weaknesses to their position that they will struggle to manage. What do you think Black's first move should be here? } 1... { -KEY- } dxe5 $1 { Black forces an exchange of pawns in the centre, knowing that all three ways of recapturing for White have their drawbacks. } 2. dxe5 { Recapturing with the pawn makes sense, seeking to avoid minor piece exchanges as the side with more space. The problem with following this general rule is that it falls short when the side with less space is able to find excellent posts for their pieces even with a more 'limited' selection of squares to choose from. } ( 2. fxe5 $2 { This move would be desirable to keep the knight on d7 restricted. However, after } Bxc1 3. Rxc1 Ne3 $19 { Black wins an exchange. } ) 
 ( 2. Nxe5 { This move would invite a trade of minor pieces, generally benefiical to the side with less space. } Nxe5 3. dxe5 Qb6 $1 { Black targets e3, a weak square created by the advance of White's central pawns. } 4. Be4 Rfd8 $1 { Renewing the threat of ...Ne3. } 5. Qb3 Rc4 $17 { Black has the much better placed pieces. } ) 
Nc5 $1 { Black uses the newly available c5 square. } 3. Bc2 { Avoiding the loss of the bishop pair, and keeping an eye on the vulnerable queenside light squares. } Qb6 { All of Black's pieces are actively placed, while White continues to have problems to solve here. In particular, the bishop on c1 has no good square to go to. } 4. Bd2 Ne6 $1 { Black wants to break down the remaining central pawns. } 5. g3 $6 { Desperate to avoid material losses, White fatally weakens the light squares around their king. } Ne3 $1 6. Bxe3 Qxe3 { Now the knight on f3 is under threat. } 7. Qd3 Qb6 { So far Black's dynamic play has induced a weakening of the White position, as well as allowed them to collect the bishop pair. Now the threat of ...Rfd8 is impossible to meet under good circumstances. For example: } 8. Nc3 Rfd8 9. Qe2 Nd4 $19 { Black will win at least an exchange next. This game showed what the 'worst-case scenario' can look like for the side with more space. They got no benefit from their extra square control, while Black was able to take full advantage of the weaknesses created by the advance of the pawns. As usual, the key for the side with less space was the activation of all their pieces, in particular the minor pieces. } 
