 { White has grabbed space in the centre, but left themselves with a weak pawn on d4 that can only be defended by pieces. Given this, can you find the powerful knight maneuver that Black should go for? } 1... { -KEY- } Nc8 $1 2. a3 N8e7 { The knight is headed to f5. } 3. O-O ( 3. Bg4 $1 { was needed, when White halts Black's plans without compromising their structure. } O-O $11 { Still, Black has no problems here and can focus on other means of undermining the space advantage. Namely, by going for either the ...b6 or ...e5 pawn break next. } ) 
 ( 3. g4 $2 { In a panic, White pushes the pawn forward in order to stop ...Nf5. However, the problem here is that now White has left themselves with very weakened dark squares on the kingside. } h5 $1 4. h3 { Necessary, in order to keep pawn control over the f5 square. } e5 $1 ( { Black switches to a pawn break strategy, taking advantage of the fact that } 5. dxe5 { is not possible because of } d4 $1 ) 
5. Ne2 { White tries to keep the centre together, but now allows Black a free hand in the centre: } e4 { Suddenly it is Black with the more serious space advantage in the centre. } 6. Bg2 hxg4 7. hxg4 Rxh1 8. Bxh1 Qd7 { Black keeps up the energetic play, constantly posing threats to the White position on each turn. } 9. Ng3 f5 $1 { Pushing the pawn, with the idea to play ...f4 at the right moment in order to break down the White centre. Play might continue: } 10. b4 a6 11. Bg2 f4 $1 12. Bxf4 Bxd4 $17 { The base of White's centre finally falls. In an amusing turn of events, it is Black that ultimately ends up with the greater central control, setting the stage for the discussion of the centre in the next chapter. } ) 
Nf5 $1 { Correct! Suddenly White is on the backfoot, due to the constant weakness of the d4 pawn. } 4. Ne2 O-O 5. Bg4 { White challenges the bothersome knight. } Nxe3 $1 { Exchanging pieces helps the side with less space, and White's dark squares are also left very weak. } 6. fxe3 Qg5 7. Nf4 Rad8 8. Kh1 h5 $1 9. Be2 ( 9. Bf3 $2 { runs into a tactic: } Nxd4 $1 10. exd4 Qxf4 $19 ) 
e5 $1 10. dxe5 Qxe5 $17 { Black stands much better, since they have dismantled White's space advantage while enjoying much greater piece activity. In this example Black was able to take advantage of White's overextension by targeting the d4 pawn. White defended it, but in the process lost the dark-squared bishop and was left with a weak pawn on e3. Finally Black destroyed the centre fully with the ...e5 pawn break, an example of 'undermining' as seen in the first few examples of this section. When you are the side with less space, the right strategy will usually involve one, or both, of two things. The first is to seek undermining moves (e. g. pawn break) and the second is to search for piece play to exploit overextension (e.g. knight maneuvers, piece exchanges). This brings us to a close for this chapter. Next up you can review the key takeaways in the chapter summary, or dive straight into the puzzle set if you prefer. } 
